1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,604
My name's Dan Pearman and I'd like to talk about the work of Pedal Power,

2
00:00:04,604 --> 00:00:07,060
a small charity based mainly in the UK.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:10,280
I'll be giving our contact details at the end

4
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:13,320
if anyone would like to find out more about how to support us.

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,440
But first, how the charity began

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,308
I got the idea of exporting bicycles to developing countries

7
00:00:20,308 --> 00:00:26,860
while I was in Ecuador, I went there in 1993 just after graduating from university.

8
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:30,930
After three years of studying, I wanted adventure.

9
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:35,131
I loved travelling, so I decided to join a voluntary organisation

10
00:00:35,131 --> 00:00:38,950
and was sent to Ecuador to carry out land surveys.

11
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:45,845
The project came to an end after five years and when I returned to the UK in 1998,

12
00:00:45,845 --> 00:00:47,760
I started planning Pedal Power.

13
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:52,060
Where I lived in Ecuador was a very rural area.

14
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:55,480
My neighbour had the only bicycle in the village

15
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,910
whereas everyone else walked everywhere.

16
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:01,400
My neighbour's business was unusually successful

17
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:04,400
and for years I couldn't understand why.

18
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,840
Then I realised having a bike meant he could get

19
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:09,810
where he wanted to go without much trouble.

20
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:13,511
Other local carpenters could only accept jobs in a three-kilometre radius,

21
00:01:13,511 --> 00:01:16,900
so no matter how skilled they were

22
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,230
they could never do as many jobs as my neighbour.

23
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:22,803
At Pedal Power, we collect second-hand bikes in the UK

24
00:01:22,803 --> 00:01:27,090
and send them to some of the poorest regions in the world.

25
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:32,120
When we distribute bikes overseas we don't give them away for free.

26
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,320
We'd like to, but long term that doesn't really help the local economy.

27
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:41,500
The demand for bikes is enormous, which makes them very expensive locally.

28
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,170
So we sell them for 5% of the normal price.

29
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:49,909
But in order to continue operating we need to have a constant supply of bikes

30
00:01:49,909 --> 00:01:52,120
which we send out every six months.

31
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:57,860
One example of a town that's received bicycles from Pedal Power is Rivas.

32
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:02,180
It was the first place I sent a full container of bicycles to

33
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:04,270
Most people there now own a bicycle.

34
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:07,480
The local economy has developed so much

35
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,790
you wouldn't recognise it as the same place.

36
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:13,680
In fact, there are more bikes than on the streets of Amsterdam

37
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:15,060
if you've ever been there.

38
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,540
But Pedal Power still needs your help.

39
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:22,500
You may have read about some of our recent problems in the British media.

40
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,330
In August 2000, we simply ran out of money.

41
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:31,240
We had containers of bikes ready to send, but no money to pay the bills

42
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:33,400
It was a terrible situation.

43
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,680
We managed to ensure the bikes went out on time

44
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:41,730
but the other problems carried on for several months.

45
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:48,000
Fortunately in October 2001 we won an Enterprise Award which helped us enormously.

46
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,560
We invested fifteen of the seventy-five-thousand-pound prize money to help secure our future.

47
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,229
Winning the award helped raise our profile,

48
00:02:57,229 --> 00:03:01,220
and the money enabled us to pay all our shipping costs

49
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:03,330
which represent our greatest expense.

50
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:08,440
Pedal Power changes lives. When someone gets a bicycle from us

51
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:11,430
they see a 14% increase in their income.

52
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:14,920
We're currently looking to invest in computers

53
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:17,790
so that our office staff can do an even better job.

54
00:03:17,790 --> 00:03:23,620
Because of our work, people in a number of countries now have a better standard of living

55
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:27,930
so far we've provided 46,000 people with bikes.

56
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:32,570
But we'd like to send more, at least 50,000 by the end of the year.

57
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:36,792
Now there are many ways in which you can support the work of Pedal Power,

58
00:03:36,792 --> 00:03:40,170
not just by taking a bike to a collection in your area.

59
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:44,578
I should also like to say if you do have a bike to donate,

60
00:03:44,578 --> 00:03:46,920
it doesn't matter what condition it's in

61
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:50,560
if we can't repair it, we'll strip it down for spare parts.

62
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,940
Of course, to do that we always need tools

63
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,580
which are expensive to buy, so we welcome any that you can give.

64
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:01,704
Also, you could help by contacting the voluntary staff at our offices,

65
00:04:01,704 --> 00:04:06,860
they'll be able to suggest activities you could organise to bring in funds for us.

66
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:11,590
People do all kinds of things-including, of course, sponsored bike rides.

67
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:18,215
Also, we're always interested to hear of other places that would benefit from receiving a consignment of bikes,

68
00:04:18,215 --> 00:04:23,000
and welcome suggestions from people who've been to developing regions on their travels.

69
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,020
We hope that by talking on radio programmes like this

70
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:29,727
we will be able to raise public awareness,

71
00:04:29,727 --> 00:04:34,211
which will lead to government organisations also giving us regular financial support,

72
00:04:34,211 --> 00:04:36,080
something that we really need.

73
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:38,420
If you'd like some more information about

74
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:42,890
where to donate an old bicycle or offer help in other ways please contact us on...
